{"tool_name":"bamsnap","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"bamsnap","home":"https://github.com/parklab/bamsnap","license":"MIT","summary":"BamSnap is a lightweight command-based visualization tool for sequencing reads in BAM files.","addition_date":"2021-03-19T08:34:30Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:37:08.461444Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:bamsnap","credit":[{"email":"peter_park@hms.harvard.edu","name":"Peter J Park","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9378-960X","typeEntity":"Person"}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://bamsnap.readthedocs.io/"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Read mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3198"},{"term":"Sequence visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0564"},{"term":"Visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0337"}]}],"name":"BamSnap","owner":"Kigaard","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btaa1101","metadata":{"abstract":"Summary: Despite the improvement in variant detection algorithms, visual inspection of the read-level data remains an essential step for accurate identification of variants in genome analysis. We developed BamSnap, an efficient BAM file viewer utilizing a graphics library and BAM indexing. In contrast to existing viewers, BamSnap can generate high-quality snapshots rapidly, with customized tracks and layout. As an example, we produced read-level images at 1000 genomic loci for >2500 whole-genomes.","authors":[{"name":"Berselli M."},{"name":"Chu C."},{"name":"Kwon M."},{"name":"Lee S."},{"name":"Park P.J."}],"citationCount":4,"date":"2021-01-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"BamSnap: A lightweight viewer for sequencing reads in BAM files"},"pmcid":"PMC8055225","pmid":"33416869"}],"topic":[{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"BamSnap","home":"https://bio.